{:edb/db-before-t 1707 :edb/db-after-t 1708 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535020 50 #inst "2026-10-08T14:52:13.501Z" 13194139535020 true] [17592186220654 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220655 13194139535020 true] [17592186220685 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220686 13194139535020 true] [17592186220689 1104 #edb/ref 17592186220691 13194139535020 true] [17592186221119 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2x - y, \\qquad y(1) = 0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right),$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535020 true] [17592186221119 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2x - y, \\qquad y(1) = 0$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)$, rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535020 false] [17592186221119 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac43\n$$\n\nwe will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2x - y + (2x_{new} - y_{new})) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(2x - y + 2x_{new} - y_{new}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(2x - y + 2x_{new}) - \\frac{1}{6}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{6}(2x - y + 2x_{new}) - \\frac{1}{6}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{6}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y) - \\frac{1}{6}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{6}y_{new} &= \\frac{1}{6}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y) \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{7}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{7}(2x + 2x_{new} + 5y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}(2 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} + 5 \\cdot 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.67\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67\n$$" 13194139535020 false] [17592186221119 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\dfrac43,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac43 - 1 = \\dfrac13.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(2x-y+2{x}_{\\text{new}})-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)-\\frac{1}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{6}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 1 + \\dfrac13 = \\dfrac43.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{7}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+5\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈0.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\!\\left(\\dfrac43\\right) \\approx 0.67.$" 13194139535020 true] [17592186221120 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x^2 - 2y, \\qquad y(3) = -1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 4.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value $x_\\text{new}.$" 13194139535020 true] [17592186221120 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 3x^2 - 2y, \\qquad y(3) = -1$\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $\\Delta x = 4$. Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_\\text{new}$, in terms of the current values $x$ and $y$, and the new value $x_\\text{new}$." 13194139535020 false] [17592186221120 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(3x^{2} - 2y + (3(x_{new})^{2} - 2y_{new})) \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + 6x_{new}^{2} - 4y - 4y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + 6x^{2} + 6x_{new}^{2} - 4y - 4y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} &= 6x^{2} + 6(x_{new})^{2} - 3y - 4y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y_{new} &= 6x^{2} + 6(x_{new})^{2} - 3y \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{5}(6x^{2} + 6(x_{new})^{2} - 3y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535020 false] [17592186221120 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{x}^{2}-2y+(3({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+6{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-4y-4{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+6{x}^{2}+6{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-4y-4{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y-4{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{\\text{new}} & =6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(6{x}^{2}+6({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535020 true] [17592186221121 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - y, \\qquad y(0) = 0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2),$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535020 true] [17592186221121 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 4x - y, \\qquad y(0) = 0$\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and $y(2)$, rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535020 false] [17592186221121 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2)$. The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-0}2 = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{2}(y^{′} + y_{new}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((4x - y) + (4x_{new} - y_{new})) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} - y) - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} - y) - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) - \\frac{1}{2}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2}y_{new} &= \\frac{1}{2}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) \\\\\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{3}(4x + 4x_{new} + y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2)$.\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0, \\qquad y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + 1 = 1\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{3}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(4 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1 + 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1, \\qquad y=1.33\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= \\frac{1}{3}(4x + 4x_{new} + y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(4 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 2 + 1.33) \\\\\n&\\approx 4.44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 1.33, \\qquad y(2) \\approx 4.44\n$$" 13194139535020 false] [17592186221121 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new}, y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-0}2 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((4x-y)+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(1)$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0, \\qquad y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 0 + 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & ≈1.33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=1, \\qquad y=1.33.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+1.33) \\\\  & ≈4.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 1.33,\n\\qquad\ny(2) \\approx 4.44.\n$$" 13194139535020 true] [17592186221377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327149 13194139535020 true] [17592186221377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327156 13194139535020 true] [17592186221377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327163 13194139535020 true] [17592186221377 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327170 13194139535020 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327177 13194139535020 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327183 13194139535020 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327189 13194139535020 true] [17592186221378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327231 13194139535020 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327195 13194139535020 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327204 13194139535020 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327213 13194139535020 true] [17592186221379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327222 13194139535020 true] [17592186221900 1017 #edb/ref 17592186220342 13194139535020 false] [17592186221900 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327143 13194139535020 true] [17592186221900 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535020 true] [17592186221900 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}-3xy,\\,y(-2)=3\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" 13194139535020 true] [17592186221900 1100 "Con