 step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x+5y)+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)+\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and $y(2).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=1,\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+9\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+9y) \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 13) \\\\  & =-124.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{3}{2})≈13.00,\\,y(2)≈-124.00.\n$$" 13194139535020 true] [17592186327214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327215 13194139535020 true] [17592186327214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327216 131