lue problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,y(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}_{\\text{new}}(3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+2) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(3\\sqrt{x}+4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(-3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}) & =\\frac{1}{2}y(3\\sqrt{x}+4) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)}{3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =-\\frac{y(3\\sqrt{x}+4)}{3\\sqrt{{x}_{\\text{new}}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(3\\sqrt{0}+4)}{3\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ≈-2.67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-2.67.$" 13194139535020 true] [17592186327232 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327233 13194139535020 true] [17592186327232 1000 #edb/ref 1759218